---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Kbd from "../components/Kbd.astro";

const title = "Why viewbus — how it works, and what it trades away";
const description =
  "The decisions behind viewbus: a local SQLite index, peeks that don't consume messages, bulk operations that handle throttling, and cheap background monitoring.";
const path = "/why-viewbus";

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  itemListElement: [
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://viewbus.app/" },
    { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Why viewbus", item: "https://viewbus.app/why-viewbus/" },
  ],
};

const techArticleSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  headline: "Why viewbus — how it works, and what it trades away",
  description,
  url: "https://viewbus.app/why-viewbus/",
  author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Håkon Lia" },
};

const receipts = [
  ["Browsing is a disk read", "the tree comes from SQLite on your machine"],
  ["Peeks don't consume", "no dequeue, no delivery-count bump"],
  ["Secrets go in the OS keychain", "not the index, not the logs"],
  ["No telemetry", "nothing about you is sent anywhere"],
];
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path={path}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbSchema)} />
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(techArticleSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-20">
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight leading-[1.05]">
      Why viewbus
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      viewbus exists because I kept losing time in the Azure Portal. Never a lot at once — half a
      minute waiting for a blade, another expanding a tree, then finding out the queue I opened wasn't
      the one I wanted. It adds up when something is on fire.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
      So the app makes a few specific bets. This page is about what they are, and what they cost.
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-8 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {receipts.map(([term, def]) => (
        <li class="flex gap-2.5">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="17" height="17" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-success)" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="mt-0.5 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5" /></svg>
          <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-medium text-[var(--color-fg)]">{term}</span>
            <span class="text-[var(--color-muted)]"> — {def}</span>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <section class="mt-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Browsing reads a local index</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        When you sign in, viewbus walks your subscriptions once and writes every namespace, queue,
        topic, and subscription into a SQLite file on your machine. Everything after that — expanding
        the tree, searching resource names, spotlight — reads from that file. It's why navigation feels
        instant on a tenant with thousands of resources.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        The cost is obvious once you say it out loud: what you're looking at is a snapshot, not a live
        feed. The message counts next to a queue in the sidebar are from the last time that namespace
        was indexed, and they can be several minutes behind. viewbus re-indexes a namespace when your
        attention lands on it — you click it, you jump to it from spotlight, you run an operation, you
        come back to the window after a while — but it skips the refresh if that namespace was already
        indexed recently. The threshold defaults to 15 minutes and is a setting you can change, or set
        to zero to turn the whole thing off.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        I went this way on purpose rather than polling on a timer, because polling burns Azure requests
        all day for numbers nobody is reading. If you want a number you can trust right now, open the
        resource: the messages themselves are always read live from the broker.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Reading messages doesn't consume them</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        This is the part I'd want to be sure about if I were installing someone else's tool against a
        production namespace. viewbus reads messages over AMQP using peek, so nothing is received and
        nothing is dequeued. Delivery counts don't move, and no message gets closer to dead-lettering
        because you looked at it. On session-enabled entities it takes a session lock while it reads and
        releases it again.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Since peeking is safe, the UI leans on it. You can page through a dead-letter queue, filter by
        session, message ID, correlation ID, subject or dead-letter reason, and search inside message
        bodies across a whole namespace rather than one entity at a time. That last one is the thing I
        wanted most and couldn't get anywhere else. It isn't free — searching bodies means actually
        reading through messages, so on a large namespace it takes a while.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Namespaces you add with a connection string behave the same as ones discovered through your
        Azure account. They're indexed into the same tables and every feature treats them identically;
        the only difference you'll notice is where they sit in the sidebar.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Fixing a message is an edit</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        When something dead-letters over one bad field, you shouldn't have to rebuild the message by
        hand. Open it, change the body or properties in a side-by-side diff, and resend. You see what
        changed before anything goes back on the bus. Composing new messages works too, with control
        over properties, content type, and scheduling.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Bulk work runs as a job</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Draining forty thousand dead letters is a different problem from draining forty. Service Bus
        will throttle you partway through, and a naive loop either gives up or keeps hammering.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        So purge, move-to-dead-letter, bulk resend, and bulk delete all run as jobs. You see which
        messages the filter matched before anything happens, then progress and an estimate while it
        runs, and you can cancel. Throttling backs off instead of failing the run. Each resource keeps a
        history of what's been run against it, which has saved me more than once when I couldn't
        remember whether I'd already emptied something.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Monitoring you can leave running</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        viewbus samples dead-letter counts in the background on a cadence you pick. Group the queues,
        topics, and subscriptions you care about into named sets, give each set a threshold — tell me if
        any production DLQ goes over 25 — and you get a notification when one trips. Anything that turns
        out to be noise can be muted for an hour or a day, and the mute expires on its own so a real
        problem doesn't stay hidden.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        It's cheap because it's only reading counts, and it runs from the app you already have open
        rather than from something you'd have to host.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Spotlight, because clicking through a tree is slow</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        <Kbd>Ctrl</Kbd> <Kbd>I</Kbd> anywhere on your machine opens a fuzzy search over every indexed
        resource. Type a few characters of a queue name, press Enter, and viewbus comes to the front on
        it. It works with the app closed to the tray, so in practice it's a launcher for your Service
        Bus topology rather than a feature you have to go and find. The hotkey is rebindable.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Two ways to automate it</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        There's an <a href="/mcp-server/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">MCP server</a>
        built in, so Claude, Cursor, or Codex can answer things like "how many dead letters landed on the
        orders topic between 11 and 3, and what do they have in common". It's read-only against your bus
        and holds no credentials of its own. Filters run inside viewbus, so message bodies don't end up
        in a model's context unless you ask for a specific one.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        For everything that isn't an AI there's a
        <a href="/developers/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">local HTTP API</a> on
        <code class="ic">127.0.0.1</code>: check status, search the index, focus a resource. It's meant
        for a Raycast or PowerToys Run command, a VS Code task, or a shell script. Both are local only —
        nothing binds to your network, and nothing routes through a server of mine.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">There's no backend to trust</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        Sign-in is an OAuth flow straight to Microsoft. Tokens and connection strings go in the Windows
        Credential Manager or the macOS Keychain, never into the index and never into a log file. The
        app's content security policy limits outbound traffic to Microsoft's login and management
        endpoints, your own <code class="ic">*.servicebus.windows.net</code> namespaces, and two static
        JSON files on this domain that it reads at startup to check for updates and notices.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        You don't have to take my word for it — open the network inspector and watch. The
        <a href="/privacy/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">privacy page</a> lists
        every file the app writes and every host it talks to.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">It's native, and it's free</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        viewbus is Rust with a system webview via Tauri, not a bundled browser. The work that matters —
        indexing, fuzzy search, AMQP, bulk jobs — happens in compiled code. It runs natively on Windows
        and on Apple Silicon.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-muted)] leading-relaxed">
        There's no paid tier, no seat count, and no account, because it's a tool I wanted to exist and
        keeping it free is easier than the alternative. If it saves you an afternoon,
        <a href="https://buymeacoffee.com/hakonlia" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">a coffee</a>
        is welcome and never expected.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-16 flex flex-col gap-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border)] pt-8 sm:flex-row sm:items-center">
      <a
        href="/download/"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-full h-11 px-6 text-sm font-medium bg-[var(--color-fg)] text-[var(--color-bg)] transition-colors hover:bg-[color-mix(in_oklab,var(--color-fg)_88%,transparent)]"
      >Download viewbus</a>
      <a
        href="/service-bus-explorer-alternative/"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-full h-11 px-6 text-sm font-medium border border-[var(--color-border)] transition-colors hover:bg-[var(--color-hover)]"
      >Coming from Service Bus Explorer?</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
